5. FUNCTIONAL TESTING - TEST LEVELS
-----------------------------------------------------
Functional testing checks WHAT the software does (does each feature
work as per requirements?).

The four levels go from small to big:

1) Unit Testing
- Tests one small function or module alone
- Done by developers, usually white box
- Example: test only the function that checks password length >= 8
- Analogy: checking one brick

2) Integration Testing
- Checks that modules work together and share data correctly
- Example: after login, does the home page show the user's name?
- Analogy: bricks and cement forming a wall

3) System Testing
- Tests the complete, fully built application end to end
- Done by the QA team, black box
- Example: register, login, search, add to cart, pay, get confirmation
- Analogy: checking the whole house

4) User Acceptance Testing (UAT)
- Final test by the client or end users to confirm business needs are met
- Done before going live. This is validation in action.
- Example: client team tests with real scenarios and signs off
- Analogy: the buyer walks through the house and accepts it

Order: Unit > Integration > System > UAT
       (Small > Together > Whole > Customer)

Summary:
Level        What is tested            Who tests            When
Unit         One function/module       Developers           First
Integration  Modules together          Developers/testers   After unit
System       Whole application         QA team              After integration
UAT          Business needs            Client/end users     Last

Note: These are officially called TEST LEVELS (Day 2 topic). Functional
testing can be done at each level.


