6. AUTOMATION TESTING
-----------------------------------------------------
Automation testing = using a tool or script (Playwright, Selenium, etc.)
to run tests instead of a person clicking by hand.

Why automate:
- Faster than manual testing
- Can run again and again, even overnight
- Fewer human mistakes
- Best for tests repeated often

1) Regression Testing
- Re-run old tests after a change or bug fix to make sure nothing that
  used to work is now broken
- Repeated every release, so a top candidate for automation
- Example: checkout page changed, so re-run login, search and cart tests

2) Smoke Testing
- Quick, basic check of main features on a new build to decide if it is
  stable enough for deeper testing
- If smoke tests fail, the build goes back to developers
- Example: does the app open, can you log in, does the home page load?

3) API Testing
- Tests the backend communication directly, without the UI
- Send a request, check status code, data and speed
- Faster and more stable than UI testing
- Example: "get user" API returns status 200 and correct user details
- (Covered in Weeks 9-10: Postman + Python Requests)

4) Data-Driven Testing
- ONE test script, MANY sets of data
- Data is kept outside the script (Excel / CSV / JSON / database)
- Example: one login test runs 50 times with 50 username/password combinations
- (Covered in Week 7, Day 48)

5) Keyword-Driven Testing
- Tests are written using keywords for actions (OpenBrowser, Login,
  ClickButton), usually in a table
- Even non-programmers can build tests; keywords link to code behind the scenes
- Less common today; data-driven is used more in pytest and Playwright

Summary:
Type            One-line meaning
Regression      Re-check old features after changes
Smoke           Quick "is the build basically working?" check
API             Test the backend directly, no UI
Data-Driven     Same test, many data sets
Keyword-Driven  Tests built from action keywords

Note: These are different kinds of things.
- Regression and Smoke = WHAT you test (purpose)
- API = WHERE you test (backend, not UI)
- Data-driven and Keyword-driven = HOW you build the automation (framework approach)


